# LeakSanitizer suppressions — hyperdht-cpp teardown only.
#
# These are DELIBERATELY narrow. Every frame named below is inside the
# dependency; nothing in holesail-cpp is suppressed, so a leak we introduce
# still fails the build. Verified 2026-07-28 against hyperdht-cpp a08cade;
# block 1 re-verified and corrected 2026-08-01 against hyperdht-cpp 81974c0.
#
# Use with:
#   LSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=test/lsan.supp
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Cancelled query state is not fully released.
#
# hyperdht_query_free() deletes only the wrapper; it does not take ownership
# of the underlying query state. Measured A/B under ASAN upstream: ~1272 B /
# 17 allocations per query cancelled in flight.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-08-01 against hyperdht-cpp 4f463c5. An earlier note here
# claimed hyperdht_query_cancel() never fires on_done, in violation of
# hyperdht.h:751, and put the leak at ~34 KB. Both were wrong. Upstream
# re-ran it standalone: on_done DOES fire with HYPERDHT_ERR_CANCELLED on all
# four cancelable paths and in both cancel/free orderings. What legitimately
# does not fire is query_free() with no preceding cancel — documented at
# hyperdht.h:759, and the likely subject of the original repro. The original
# measurement was also distorted by a buffering trap: stderr DHT_LOG is
# unbuffered while piped stdout is block-buffered, so a callback that did
# fire can look like it never did.
#
# Because cancel DOES fire on_done, HolesailClient::destroy() clears query_
# BEFORE calling cancel — see the comment at src/client.cpp.
leak:hyperdht_mutable_get_ex
leak:hyperdht_immutable_get_ex
leak:hyperdht::query::Query

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Baseline teardown leak, ~1237 bytes / 4 allocations per DHT.
#
# Reproducible with a program containing no holesail code at all, following
# the create -> listen -> destroy -> uv_run -> free recipe from hyperdht.h
# verbatim. It leaves one stopped-but-unclosed uv_timer_t in the loop
# (active=0 closing=0 ref=1), which is also why uv_loop_close() cannot
# return 0 after using a DHT.
leak:hyperdht_create
leak:hyperdht::HyperDHT::HyperDHT
leak:hyperdht::HyperDHT::start_interface_watcher
leak:on_interface_event_interval
leak:uv_interface_addresses

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Connect/stream state dropped at teardown, ~11 KB per run once a test
#    actually establishes connections.
#
# Added after the integration suite became the first thing here to make real
# connections. Every allocation was traced to its #1 frame and all of them
# are inside the dependency: hyperdht drops in-flight connect and stream
# state when hyperdht_destroy lands before a graceful stream close has
# round-tripped. The count scales with the number of connections, which is
# consistent with that reading. Same class as defects 1 and 2 above and
# likewise belongs upstream.
leak:hyperdht::HyperDHT::do_connect
leak:hyperdht::server::Server::on_handshake_result
leak:udx__cirbuf_init
leak:udx__cirbuf_set

# The uv_loop_t's own internals, leaked strictly as a CONSEQUENCE of defect 2:
# uv_loop_close() returns EBUSY because of hyperdht's orphaned timer, so libuv
# never frees them. Not a separate bug.
leak:uv_loop_init
leak:uv__io_start
